==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|swerdą||literally ‘pricking, piercing thing’|lang=gem-pro}}, of uncertain origin, but most likely ultimately from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|swer-||‘fester, suppurate’ later ‘pierce, wound’|lang=ine-pro}}.
Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|swerd|lang=ofs}} (West Frisian {{term|swurd|lang=fy}}), Old Saxon {{term|swerd|lang=osx}} (Low German {{term|Sweerd|lang=nds}}), Old Dutch {{term|swert|lang=odt}} (Dutch ''[[zwaard]])'', Old High German {{term|swert|lang=goh}} (German ''[[Schwert]]''), Old Norse {{term|sverð|lang=non}} (Danish ''[[sværd]]'', Norwegian ''[[sverd]]'', Swedish ''[[svärd]])''

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/sweord/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
